Paper Summary:
The paper investigates the internal representations of autoregressive GUI agents, identifying a "coordinate serialization bottleneck" where intermediate transformer layers hold optimal spatial grounding signals, while final layers specialize in coordinate token formatting. To exploit this, the authors propose ZwerGe-UI, a parameter-efficient, frozen-backbone retrofit. It extracts layer-wise spatial posteriors using intermediate cross-attention probes and dynamically aggregates them via a cross-layer fusion head. Evaluated on GUI benchmarks, ZwerGe-UI improves spatial grounding over standard autoregressive decoding.

Summary Of Strengths:
The isolation of the spatial-to-serialization transition using dual analytical lenses (spatial probes vs. teacher-forced coordinate log-likelihoods) provides insights into MLLM behavior.

Freezing the backbone and appending shallow cross-attention probes (~0.5% of backbone parameters) could improve efficiency.

The instruction-switch experiment rules out passive visual saliency, providing potential evidence that the mid-layer signals are instruction-conditioned spatial posteriors.

Summary Of Weaknesses:
I am a little confused by the CosMeta fusion design (Eq. 5). The text correctly notes the "heterogeneity of semantic spaces" across different depths (Section 3.1). Could the authors clarify how a single global, learnable meta-query (
) can effectively compute cosine similarities across these heterogeneous latent spaces?

Could you elaborate further on the behavior of the anisotropic Gaussian label (Eq. 3) for very small targets? Since the variance is defined as a fraction of the bounding box size (
, 
), it seems the variance might approach zero for tiny icons on high-resolution screens. I wonder if the authors could discuss whether this causes the KL divergence loss (Eq. 4) to revert to a hard, one-hot penalty in these exact scenarios, which is the specific issue soft-labeling aims to mitigate.

Have the authors considered the potential impact of an out-of-distribution (OOD) shift during prefill-forced inference? The backbone agents (e.g., Qwen-VL variants) are typically trained to generate <|ground|><|pointer_start|> tokens autoregressively, often after latent reasoning or Chain-of-Thought tokens.

What was the justification for applying a strict 
 pre-scale constraint (
) prior to LayerNorm (Section 3.1)? While this effectively prevents numerical overflow, in modern LLMs, activation magnitude often correlates with token confidence or attention sinks.

It is not entirely clear how the continuous KL divergence training objective fully aligns with the discrete inference decoding strategy. While the model is trained to map a unimodal Gaussian posterior, inference relies on a "BFS centroid decode strategy with a 0.3x max threshold" (Section 4.1).

